# Git & Release Workflow

## Before first code edit (fixes & features) — REQUIRED

Use this for any **bugfix**, **feature**, or **multi-file / non-trivial** implementation. Skip only for **read-only** tasks or when the user explicitly says “no branch” / “edit on `main` only”.

**Always create a new branch for implementation work** — never edit on `main`, and do not pile unrelated changes onto some other open `feat/*`/`fix/*` branch. Reuse an existing topic branch only when the user explicitly asks to continue that branch.

1. **Do not** use repo-editing tools (`apply_patch`, scripted bulk edits, etc.) until you are on a **`fix/<slug>`** or **`feat/<slug>`** branch — not on `main` (see also: *Never commit fixes or features directly on `main`* under canary flow below).
2. **ALWAYS branch from the current HEAD** — never switch to `main` just to branch:
   - `git checkout -b fix/<short-slug>` **or** `git checkout -b feat/<short-slug>` directly from wherever you are.
   - Do **not** `git checkout main && git pull` beforehand. Switching to `main` first would drop commits from the current topic branch and cause divergence. The new branch inherits the current HEAD's commit history.
   - Only branch from `main` when the user explicitly says "start fresh from main" or when you are already on `main`.
3. **Uncommitted changes:** run **`git checkout -b fix/<slug>`** (or `feat/<slug>`) *before* continuing edits so work is not stacked on the current branch. If you must switch branches with a dirty tree and Git blocks you, **`git stash push`**, create the branch, then **`git stash pop`**.
4. **Verify before editing:** `git branch --show-current` — must **not** be `main` (unless the user explicitly asked to work on `main`).

## Branch cleanup — one active branch at a time

At most **one** `feat/*` or `fix/*` branch should exist for a given line of work. Avoid accumulating stale branches.

### When creating a new branch that supersedes a previous one

If the new branch is created from HEAD and the old branch's commits are already included in that HEAD:

1. Delete the **old** branch locally and on `origin`:
   ```bash
   git branch -D <old-branch>
   git push origin --delete <old-branch>
   ```
2. Verify: `git branch -a | grep <old-branch>` should return nothing.

### After merging to `main`

Once a feature or fix branch has been merged into `main`, delete that branch locally and on `origin` (no need to wait for GA — cleanup is immediate). The "After GA" section below has the detailed commands.

## Planning (Plan mode / implementation plans)

When producing or updating an **implementation plan** (e.g. Cursor Plan mode / `CreatePlan`), include a short **Git workflow** subsection aligned with *Before first code edit* above: **always** `git checkout -b` a **new** `feat/<slug>` or `fix/<slug>` from the current HEAD before any code edits, never plan work as if it will land directly on `main`, and note that **commit / push / tag** require an explicit in-turn user instruction (*Agent commit policy* below).

## Agent commit policy (STRICT)

**Never run `git commit`, `git tag`, or `git push` without an explicit, in-turn
user instruction.** This overrides any "keep triggering canary builds in a
loop" style request once debugging is in progress — multiple fixes may need
to be stacked before they are worth a new commit/tag.

- Default behavior while iterating: edit files, run type-checks / unit tests,
  and surface the diff (e.g. `git status`, `git diff --stat`) for review.
- Only proceed with `git add` / `git commit` / `git tag` / `git push` when the
  user explicitly says something like "commit it", "tag canary.N", "push", or
  "ship it". Approval must be given in the current turn; earlier blanket
  approvals do not carry over across newly-discovered bugs.
- Never auto-bump the canary number between fixes. Wait for the user to
  confirm that the fix is ready to ship.
- If you believe a commit is warranted, **ask first** instead of acting.

## Commits

- Commit messages must be prefixed with a **gitmoji** (e.g. `🐛 fix(scope): …`, `✨ feat(scope): …`).
- Before merge: show `git status` and `git branch -a`.
- After merge/push: run `git fetch --prune` to confirm remote branch cleanup.
- After merge: verify with `git log --oneline -3`.
- Before `git stash push/pop`: run `git stash list`.

## Release triggers

Docker builds run **only on tag pushes**, not on commits to `main`.
`.github/workflows/docker-release.yml` reacts to:

- `v*.*.*` → pushes `lqdflying/lobehub:X.Y.Z` **and** `:latest` (GA only)
- `v*.*.*-canary.*` → pushes `lqdflying/lobehub:X.Y.Z-canary.N` (does **not** touch `:latest`)

Version scheme: `v3.x.x` (LobeHub independent versioning, unrelated to upstream v1.x).

**Docker build reliability:** `.github/workflows/docker-release.yml` frees unused runner disk (preinstalled SDK trees, `docker system prune`) before `docker build` so large Next.js images are less likely to hit **“No space left on device”** on `ubuntu-latest`.

## Fix / feature canary flow (default)

Use this sequence **only when the user explicitly asks to ship / tag / cut a
canary**. Never kick it off on your own — the STRICT policy above wins for
both canary and formal tags.

**Never commit fixes or features directly on `main` just to get an image.**

1. Branch off `main`: `fix/<slug>` or `feat/<slug>`.
2. Commit on the branch with a gitmoji prefix. **Do not** bump `package.json`.
3. Pick the canary version — always a pre-release of the **next unreleased** version, never the current GA:
   - Read `package.json` version (= last GA, e.g. `3.4.38`).
   - If `git tag -l vX.Y.Z` matches → that GA shipped → canary = `vX.Y.(Z+1)-canary.N`.
   - Otherwise canary = `vX.Y.Z-canary.N`.
   - `N` = (highest existing `vX.Y.Z-canary.*` for that version) + 1:
     `git tag -l 'vX.Y.Z-canary.*' | sort -V | tail`
4. Push branch, then tag:
   ```bash
   git push -u origin <branch>
   git tag vX.Y.Z-canary.N
   git push origin vX.Y.Z-canary.N
   ```
5. Verify the build on the **fork**, not upstream:
   ```bash
   gh run list -R lqdflying/lobehub --workflow=docker-release.yml --limit 3
   ```
6. Do not merge the branch to `main` until the canary image has been tested.

## Recover from a wrong tag

```bash
gh run cancel <run-id> -R lqdflying/lobehub
git push --delete origin vBAD && git tag -d vBAD
git tag vGOOD && git push origin vGOOD
```

## GA promotion (only after canary passes)

Trigger only on explicit user instruction (e.g. "promote to GA", "bump to
vX.Y.Z and tag"). Do not auto-promote even when the canary looks green.

**Typical flow:** merge the feature branch into **`main`** (fast-forward or merge commit), then on **`main`** bump **`package.json`**, commit, tag **`vX.Y.Z`**, push **`main`** and the tag. Ensure `main` contains all GA work before tagging.

```bash
# On main after merge — replace old/new version strings
sed -i 's/"version": "old"/"version": "new"/' package.json
git add package.json && git commit -m "🔖 chore: bump version to vX.Y.Z"
git tag vX.Y.Z && git push origin main && git push origin vX.Y.Z
```

(This replaces older one-liners that pushed `HEAD:main` from a non-main branch — prefer **checkout `main`**, merge, bump, tag from **`main`**.)

This is the **only** flow allowed to push a non-canary `v*.*.*` tag and update `:latest`.

### GitHub Release (MANDATORY — part of GA)

Every GA tag **must** be accompanied by a `gh release create`. The release description must summarize all changes since the previous GA release.

1. Find the **previous GA tag**:
   ```bash
   gh release list -R lqdflying/lobehub --limit 5
   # or: git tag -l 'v*' --sort=-v:refname | grep -v 'canary' | head -2
   ```
2. Generate the **release notes** by reviewing all commits between the previous GA and the new tag:
   ```bash
   git log --oneline vPREV..vNEW
   ```
3. Create the release:
   ```bash
   gh release create vX.Y.Z -R lqdflying/lobehub \
     --title "vX.Y.Z — <short summary>" \
     --notes "$(cat <<'EOF'
   ## Highlights

   - summarized from git log between previous GA and this tag
   - group by category (Bug Fix, Feature, etc.)

   ## What's Changed

   (details...)

   ---

   Full diff: https://github.com/lqdflying/lobehub/compare/vPREV...vNEW
   EOF
   )"
   ```

Do **not** create the release before pushing the tag — the tag push triggers the Docker build. Create the release after the tag push.

## After GA: delete unused branches

Once a feature or fix branch has been **merged into `main`** and the GA tag has shipped, **delete that branch locally and on `origin`** so the repo stays tidy. Do this **only when the user asks** (e.g. “clean up branches”, “delete merged branches”) or as part of an explicit GA follow-up.

**Do not delete** `main`, release-protection branches, or anything the user still uses for open work.

### Check what is safe to remove

```bash
git fetch --prune origin
git checkout main && git pull origin main
git branch --merged main    # local branches fully merged into main (except main itself)
```

Optional: compare to remote feature branches — if the merge is on `main`, the remote copy of the feature branch is redundant.

### Delete locally

```bash
git branch -d <branch>      # preferred when Git agrees it is merged
git branch -D <branch>      # only if work is already on main but Git refuses -d (e.g. stray tracking mismatch)
```

### Delete on `origin`

```bash
git push origin --delete <branch> [<branch> ...]
```

### Verify

```bash
git fetch --prune origin
git branch -a
```
